      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194326/assets/a93150906c0681b8654370267fb918076338fa8ab5bf68f50a0062a4ab34af1d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=-1.$ Applying the above formula with $x=-1$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{f(-1)-f(-1-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-1)-f(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
